# Architecture Decision Records

ADRs are immutable decision records. Corrections use a new superseding ADR; accepted text is not
silently rewritten. The status written inside an ADR reports what was recorded then; it is not
edited when a later ADR changes current applicability.

Use the machine-readable [`index.json`](index.json) to answer what is effective now. Each entry
records:

- `id`, `title`, `path`, and `date` for identity and navigation;
- `date_source`, which is `adr` when the document declares a date and `git-introduction` when the
  catalog must use the file's historical introduction date;
- `recorded_status`: `Proposed`, `Accepted`, `Superseded`, or `Rejected` from immutable ADR text;
- `effective_status`: `proposed`, `effective`, `partially-superseded`, `superseded`, or `rejected`;
- reciprocal `supersedes` and `superseded_by` relationships; and
- `scope: partial|full` plus a description that prevents a partial change from making an entire
  ADR appear ineffective.

The [current governing specification](../../governing-specification.md) is the design entry point.
[ADR-0062](ADR-0062-typed-authority-and-specification-lifecycle.md) defines the typed authority
model and the recorded-status/effective-status split. Semantic checks require one catalog entry for
every ADR file and fail on invalid identities, statuses, dates, paths, or nonreciprocal
supersession.
